"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 27. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 3), pathological T3a stage, positive surgical margins, and absence of seminal vesicle invasion or lymph node metastasis. The biopsy revealed a Gleason 3+4 pattern, which differs from the prostatectomy specimen. The absence of lymph node metastasis is a favorable finding.\n\nCountervailing factors include the positive surgical margins and the higher preoperative PSA. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding the subsequent trajectory. The nodal status was documented as no lymph node metastasis. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its subsequent trend would be crucial in revising this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 42.09 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 31.57–52.61 months; its coverage has not been validated."
